#============================================================================
# NETWORK CAPABILITY LIBRARY — PURE SYMBOLIC SPECIFICATION
# Date: 2026-03-27
# Purpose: Complete enumeration of all networking operations
# Format: Pure algebraic/symbolic (no code, no algorithms)
# ZEROPOINT Compliance Target: 70/70 (all five gates pass)
# Bidirectional Mirror: multiuser_operations (15 dual pairs, expecting 12 network operations)
#============================================================================

## SECTION 1: SYMBOLS & NOTATION
##
## All NETWORK operations use these primitive symbols:
##
##   ⊙ = operation marker (thing network does)
##   : = operation classifier (what tier/type)
##   → = flow/transformation (data moving across network)
##   ⇄ = bidirectional (two-way sync)
##   ✓ = successful transmission
##   ✗ = transmission failed or out-of-order
##   τ = time reference (timestamp, latency)
##   μ = metric reference (bandwidth, packet loss, jitter)
##   λ = lambda/parameter reference
##   ≡ = equivalence/hash check
##   ⊗ = XOR/checksum operation
##

---

## SECTION 2: THE PRIMITIVE
##
## One binary choice underlies all network operations:
##
##   LOCAL STATE vs DISTRIBUTED STATE (synchronized across network)
##
## Every operation either:
##   (A) Operates on single-machine state locally, OR
##   (B) Replicates/synchronizes/resolves conflicts across multiple machines
##
## This choice determines the election outcome (same primitive as multiuser, different implementation).

---

## SECTION 3: TIER 1 OPERATIONS — NETWORK CONNECTIVITY (<1ms local, variable latency remote)
##
## Fast local operations. Remote calls have natural latency. No ledger creation at this tier.

⊙:connect_peer
  Input: peer_id str, peer_address str (IP:PORT), transport str (tcp|udp|ws), auth_token str
  Output: connection {peer_id, peer_address, connected_at τ, status (connected|disconnected|error)}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Establish network connection to peer. Verify address reachable, auth valid.
  Time: <1ms (local validation) | 10-100ms (actual connection, depends on network)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, cached connectivity state)
  Reverse Causality: Peer address and auth rules exist in spec BEFORE connection attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches TCP/UDP socket connection pattern
    2. Clarity: Connection either established or not (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Connection attempt logged to ledger_network_events.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Clear error message if connection fails (host unreachable, auth failed, timeout)
    5. Scaling: O(1) per peer connection, thousands of concurrent peers supported

⊙:disconnect_peer
  Input: peer_id str, reason str (optional: graceful|timeout|error)
  Output: disconnection {peer_id, disconnected_at τ, reason}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Close connection to peer. Graceful or forced.
  Time: <1ms (local) | 10ms (send close message to peer)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, state change only)
  Reverse Causality: Disconnect procedure defined BEFORE disconnect attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches socket close pattern
    2. Clarity: Connection either closed or still open (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Disconnection logged
    4. Kindness: Peer is notified of disconnection, not left hanging
    5. Scaling: O(1) per disconnection

⊙:send_message
  Input: peer_id str, message_id str, payload {}, priority (normal|high|low), sequence_num int
  Output: sent {peer_id, message_id, sent_at τ, sequence_num, status (sent|failed|timeout)}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Send message to peer. Attach sequence number for ordering.
  Time: <1ms (enqueue) | 10-100ms (transmission, depends on network)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, cache message in outbound queue)
  Reverse Causality: Message format and sequence numbering defined BEFORE send attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches message queue / packet transmission pattern
    2. Clarity: Message either sent or failed (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Send attempt logged to ledger_network_events.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Clear reason for failure (timeout, disconnected, invalid peer)
    5. Scaling: O(1) per message, millions of messages per second supported

⊙:receive_message
  Input: peer_id str (optional, if none: from any peer), timeout_ms int (default 5000)
  Output: message {peer_id, message_id, payload, sequence_num, received_at τ}, ✓|✗ (timeout)
  Effect: Wait for incoming message from peer (or any peer). Return when received or timeout.
  Time: 0-timeout_ms (depends on message arrival)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, reading inbound queue)
  Reverse Causality: Message receive handler defined BEFORE receive attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches socket read / message queue pattern
    2. Clarity: Message received or timeout occurred (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Receive attempt logged (for debugging)
    4. Kindness: Clear timeout message if wait exceeded
    5. Scaling: O(1) per receive, millions of concurrent receivers supported

⊙:list_connected_peers
  Input: filter {status: (connected|disconnected), transport: str} (optional)
  Output: peers [] {peer_id, peer_address, connected_at τ, status, latency_ms int}, count int
  Effect: Return all currently connected (or filtered) peers
  Time: <5ms (cache lookup)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, read-only)
  Reverse Causality: Peer list structure defined BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard peer discovery query
    2. Clarity: Returns all matching peers
    3. Visibility: Query logged
    4. Kindness: User can see network topology
    5. Scaling: O(k) where k = number of connected peers (typically <10,000)

⊙:measure_latency
  Input: peer_id str
  Output: latency_measurement {peer_id, round_trip_time_ms int, measured_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Send ping to peer, measure round-trip time
  Time: <1ms (send) | 10-1000ms (actual ping, depends on network distance)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, cache latency in peer metadata)
  Reverse Causality: Ping procedure defined BEFORE measurement attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches ICMP ping / TCP SYN-ACK pattern
    2. Clarity: Latency is single number in milliseconds
    3. Visibility: Measurement logged
    4. Kindness: Clear timeout if peer unreachable
    5. Scaling: O(1) per measurement

---

## SECTION 4: TIER 2 OPERATIONS — REPLICATION & SYNCHRONIZATION (<10ms decision logic, variable network latency)
##
## Cached decision operations on replication strategies. No ledger creation at this tier.

⊙:replicate_state
  Input: state_id str, state_data {}, target_peers [] (or all if empty), strategy (full|delta|merkle)
  Output: replication {state_id, target_peers, strategy, sent_at τ, bytes_sent int, status (sent|partial|failed)}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Send state to target peers using specified strategy (full copy, deltas only, or merkle tree)
  Time: <10ms (decide strategy + prepare data) | 10-1000ms (transmission, depends on data size and network)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached replication decision)
  Reverse Causality: Replication strategy defined BEFORE replication attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches database replication pattern (MySQL, PostgreSQL, Cassandra)
    2. Clarity: Strategy is one of three (full|delta|merkle), no ambiguity
    3. Visibility: Replication logged to ledger_replication_audit.jsonl (first call creates)
    4. Kindness: Automatically chooses efficient strategy based on state size
    5. Scaling: O(1) per replication decision, millions of state objects supported

⊙:request_state_from_peer
  Input: peer_id str, state_id str, version_hash str (optional), timeout_ms int
  Output: state_data {}, received_at τ, source_peer_id, ✓|✗
  Effect: Request state from peer. If version_hash provided, request only if peer has different version.
  Time: <10ms (query peer) | 10-1000ms (transmission)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: State request protocol defined BEFORE request attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches client-server pull replication
    2. Clarity: State either received or request failed (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Request logged
    4. Kindness: Version hash allows skipping unneeded transfers
    5. Scaling: O(1) per request

⊙:detect_version_divergence
  Input: state_id str, local_version_hash str, peer_states [] {peer_id, peer_version_hash}
  Output: divergence_report {state_id, divergent_peers [], are_all_same bool, most_common_version str}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Compare version hashes across all peers. Detect if state has diverged.
  Time: <5ms (hash comparison)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: Version comparison strategy defined BEFORE detection attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Dynamo/Cassandra vector clock conflict detection
    2. Clarity: Divergence either detected or not (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Divergence logged
    4. Kindness: Clear which peers have different versions
    5. Scaling: O(n) where n = number of peers (typically <10,000)

⊙:rank_peer_versions
  Input: state_id str, peer_versions [] {peer_id, version_hash, version_timestamp τ, peer_latency_ms int}
  Output: ranked_versions [] {peer_id, version_hash, rank int, reason str}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Given multiple peer versions, rank by freshness (timestamp), network quality (latency), or other criteria
  Time: <5ms (ranking logic)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: Version ranking strategy defined BEFORE ranking attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Dynamo's sloppy quorum / vector clock consensus
    2. Clarity: Versions ranked by explicit criteria
    3. Visibility: Ranking criteria logged
    4. Kindness: Clear which version is "best" and why
    5. Scaling: O(n) where n = peer versions (typically <10)

---

## SECTION 5: TIER 3 OPERATIONS — CONFLICT RESOLUTION & CONSENSUS (<50ms, creates ledgers)
##
## First call creates ledger. Resolves conflicts between diverged versions using CRDT or OT.

⊙:resolve_version_conflict
  Input: state_id str, conflicting_versions [] {peer_id, version_hash, content, last_update τ}, merge_strategy (last-write-wins|CRDT|OT|manual)
  Output: resolved_content {}, resolved_version_hash str, conflict_record {state_id, conflicting_versions, resolution_method, resolved_at τ, result}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Merge conflicting versions using specified strategy. Append to ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl
  Time: <50ms (merge algorithm + hash computation)
  Ledger: ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Merge strategy (last-write-wins, CRDT, OT) defined in spec BEFORE conflict resolution attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Git merge / Google Docs collaborative editing
    2. Clarity: Conflict is resolved deterministically (same inputs = same output)
    3. Visibility: Every conflict resolution in ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl with full history
    4. Kindness: Both versions preserved if using CRDT/OT, no silent data loss
    5. Scaling: O(c) where c = conflict size (typically <1MB)

⊙:broadcast_resolved_version
  Input: state_id str, resolved_content {}, resolved_version_hash str, target_peers [] (or all if empty)
  Output: broadcast {state_id, resolved_version_hash, broadcasted_at τ, peers_received int, bytes_sent int}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Send resolved version to all peers so they converge on same state
  Time: <50ms (prepare + send to all peers in parallel)
  Ledger: ledger_replication_audit.jsonl (append resolved version broadcast)
  Reverse Causality: Broadcast procedure defined BEFORE broadcast attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches gossip protocol / epidemic broadcast
    2. Clarity: Broadcast either sent or failed (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Broadcast logged with which peers received
    4. Kindness: All peers converge to same state
    5. Scaling: O(p) where p = number of peers

⊙:await_peer_consensus
  Input: state_id str, resolved_version_hash str, required_peers [] (peer IDs that must ack), timeout_ms int
  Output: consensus {state_id, resolved_version_hash, peers_acked [] {peer_id, acked_at τ}, quorum_reached bool, consensus_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Wait for required peers to acknowledge they have resolved version. Build quorum.
  Time: 10-timeout_ms (depends on peer responses)
  Ledger: ledger_consensus_audit.jsonl (created on first call, append-only with each peer's ack)
  Reverse Causality: Quorum/consensus rules defined BEFORE awaiting consensus
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Raft / Paxos quorum consensus
    2. Clarity: Quorum either reached or not (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Every peer's ack logged with timestamp
    4. Kindness: Clear which peers confirmed, which are missing
    5. Scaling: O(q) where q = quorum size (typically <100)

⊙:record_network_event
  Input: event_type str (sync|conflict|latency_spike|peer_down|peer_up), peer_id str, metadata {}
  Output: event_record {event_type, peer_id, metadata, recorded_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Record network event to ledger_network_events.jsonl for debugging and analysis
  Time: <5ms (ledger append)
  Ledger: ledger_network_events.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Network event types defined in spec BEFORE event recorded
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches application telemetry / observability pattern
    2. Clarity: Event is immutable record
    3. Visibility: All network events in ledger
    4. Kindness: Clear event description helps debugging
    5. Scaling: O(1) per event, millions of events supported

---

## SECTION 6: TIER 4 OPERATIONS — END-TO-END SYNCHRONIZATION WORKFLOWS (<200ms, composition of lower tiers)
##
## Composite operations for full synchronization scenarios.

⊙:full_sync_with_peers
  Input: user_id str, state_id str, local_state_hash str, all_peers [] {peer_id, peer_address}, timeout_ms int
  Output: sync_result {user_id, state_id, final_version_hash, peers_synced [], conflicts_resolved int, sync_duration_ms int, sync_record {sync_at τ}}, ✓|✗
  Effect: [measure_latency(all_peers)] → [detect_version_divergence] → [if diverged: resolve_version_conflict] → [replicate_state(resolved)] → [await_peer_consensus]
  Time: <200ms (5 sub-operations in sequence)
  Ledger: ledger_replication_audit.jsonl + ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl (append from sub-operations)
  Reverse Causality: Full sync workflow defined BEFORE full_sync_with_peers called
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Syncthing / Resilio Sync end-to-end synchronization
    2. Clarity: Sync either succeeded or failed with specific reason
    3. Visibility: Full sync audit trail across multiple ledgers
    4. Kindness: Automatic conflict resolution, user notified of any conflicts
    5. Scaling: O(p*c) where p = peers, c = conflict size

⊙:watch_state_changes
  Input: user_id str, state_id str, peer_id str, callback str (function name in client code)
  Output: watcher_record {user_id, state_id, peer_id, watching_since τ, status (active|stopped)}, ✓|✗
  Effect: [create listener] → [subscribe to peer's updates] → [on each update from peer: call callback and log to ledger_watch_audit.jsonl]
  Time: <10ms (setup) | 0-∞ (running in background)
  Ledger: ledger_watch_audit.jsonl (created on first call, append for each update received)
  Reverse Causality: Watch procedure defined BEFORE watching started
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches database transaction log tailing / event streaming (Kafka, Postgres LISTEN)
    2. Clarity: Watcher either active or stopped (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Every update from peer logged
    4. Kindness: Automatic notifications when peer's state changes
    5. Scaling: O(1) per watch, millions of concurrent watchers supported

⊙:handle_network_partition
  Input: partition_detected_peers [] (peers that became unreachable), remaining_peers [] (peers still reachable)
  Output: partition_response {partition_detected_at τ, partition_resolved_at τ (or null if still partitioned), action_taken str, recovered_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: [update peer list] → [if quorum remains: continue with remaining peers] → [if quorum lost: stop writes, go read-only] → [wait for partition to heal] → [if partition heals: full_sync_with_peers to catch up] → [record to ledger_network_partition.jsonl]
  Time: Depends on partition duration (could be seconds to hours)
  Ledger: ledger_network_partition.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Network partition handling procedure defined BEFORE partition occurs
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Dynamo / Cassandra partition tolerance behavior
    2. Clarity: System either has quorum or doesn't (boolean), state is read-only or read-write
    3. Visibility: Full partition lifecycle in ledger_network_partition.jsonl
    4. Kindness: System survives network issues, automatically recovers when partition heals
    5. Scaling: Works with any number of peers, graceful degradation

⊙:peer_health_monitoring
  Input: peers [] {peer_id, peer_address}, check_interval_ms int (default 5000), failure_threshold int (default 3)
  Output: health_report {healthy_peers [], unhealthy_peers [] {peer_id, failures_in_row int, last_failure_at τ}, timestamp τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: [periodically measure_latency for each peer] → [if latency spiked: record to ledger_peer_health.jsonl] → [if failures exceed threshold: mark unhealthy] → [if unhealthy peer recovers: mark healthy again]
  Time: check_interval_ms (runs in background)
  Ledger: ledger_peer_health.jsonl (created on first call, append for each health event)
  Reverse Causality: Health check rules defined BEFORE monitoring started
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Kubernetes health checks / database connection pooling
    2. Clarity: Peer either healthy or unhealthy (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Every health event logged
    4. Kindness: System automatically detects failing peers, avoids them
    5. Scaling: O(p) where p = number of peers, runs in background

