## SECTION 7: CONSISTENCY & AUDIT
##
## Verification operations to ensure network integrity.

⊙:verify_replication_consistency
  Input: state_id str, timeout_ms int
  Output: consistency_report {state_id, all_peers_have_same_version bool, mismatched_peers [] {peer_id, version_hash}, report_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Query all peers for state hash. Verify all have same version. Log to ledger_consistency_audit.jsonl
  Time: <100ms (query all peers in parallel + aggregate)
  Ledger: ledger_consistency_audit.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Consistency verification procedure defined BEFORE verification attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches database replication verification (MySQL compare_table_checksum)
    2. Clarity: Consistency either verified or violations found (boolean)
    3. Visibility: All mismatches logged
    4. Kindness: Automatic detection of replication problems
    5. Scaling: O(p) where p = number of peers

⊙:audit_network_events
  Input: filter {event_type: str, peer_id: str, since τ, until τ, limit int}
  Output: audit_records [] {timestamp τ, event_type, peer_id, metadata}, count int
  Effect: Query ledger_network_events.jsonl + ledger_replication_audit.jsonl + ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl for matching events
  Time: <100ms (filter across multiple ledgers)
  Ledger: None (read-only, multiple ledgers queried)
  Reverse Causality: Audit structure defined BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches audit log pattern
    2. Clarity: Events ordered by timestamp
    3. Visibility: Complete network audit trail
    4. Kindness: User can investigate network issues
    5. Scaling: O(n) linear scan acceptable

